+  "problem": "## Observed\n\n`badf-git`'s recovery contract (`references/recovery-contract.md`) opens with one rule — `PRESERVE → IDENTIFY → CLASSIFY → RECOVER → VERIFY → RECONCILE`; \"never start with `reset --hard`, destructive `clean`, forced protected-ref movement, or deletion of unknown branches/worktrees merely because the repository is inconvenient\" — and says that when state is unknown, recovery **stops mutation until it is classified**, listing the ten questions to answer first (which worktree, which ref, what is HEAD, what is staged/unstaged/untracked, which commits exist only locally, what is authoritative, is any content the only copy, is another actor relying on the branch/worktree, what authority covers the recovery, which evidence goes stale). `GIT-O5` requires \"inventory/preservation of unique state\" and \"evidence of the before state\" before any exception. Measured after GIT-F: **nothing produces that inventory, nothing preserves anything, and the recovery trigger GIT-F introduced — a `BLOCKED` reconcile — ends with the words \"open recovery\" and no tooling behind them.** In this program the \"preservation\" practised so far was a hand-typed `git branch backup/...` before every rebase; the `test_badf_integrity` residue that blocked a rebase earlier today was exactly unclassified unique state met with a hand-run `git checkout --`. GTGM v0.1 §12 (reverse/rollback/recovery contract), §15 (`git-recovery` subskill), §17 **GIT-G — Recovery**.\n\n## Expected\n\n**Preservation and classification become measured records; nothing destructive is added.**\n\n1. **`badf_gate.py git-recovery [<path>]`** (read-only, `GIT-O0`) renders one JSON **git-recovery record** — the before-state evidence the contract requires: the `git-baseline` identity (reused: worktree, HEAD, target, ahead/behind, counts) plus the **unique-state inventory**: uncommitted changes (counts only), stash entries, **dangling commits** (reflog entries of HEAD unreachable from any ref — the 